WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.260
British Prime Minister Theresa May has faced
heavy opposition in parliament, after delivering

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.260
a statement on her government's draft agreement
on post-Brexit relations with Brussels.

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:04.260
Opposition leader Jeremy Corby said the deal
represents "the worst of all worlds."

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.260
The UK and European Union appear to have settled
their differences over Brexit.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:06.260
Disputes over fishing rights, Gibraltar and
the border between Britain and Ireland are

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.260
still to be resolved.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.260
But EU leaders say they're reached agreement
in principle.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:09.260
British prime minister Theresa May is under
intense pressure from political opponents

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.260
at home, but says she's confident British
lawmakers will approve the deal.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.260
They’re not exactly celebrating, but EU
leaders including chief Brexit negotiator

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.260
Jean-Michel Barnier have at least one reason
to be cheerful.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.460
The draft agreement on the UK's withdrawal
from the bloc should be signed off at a summit

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.570
in Brussels on Sunday.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:29.119
British prime minister Theresa May told parliament
in London it was a

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:32.599
good deal for Britain.

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:39.431
It honours the vote of the British people
by taking back control of our borders, our

00:00:39.431 --> 00:00:44.760
laws and our money, while protecting jobs,
security and the integrity of our precious

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.839
United Kingdom.

00:00:46.839 --> 00:01:01.190
It ends free movement once and 
for all.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:16.140
We will make our own laws in our own parliaments.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.790
And it means an end to sending vast sums of
money to the EU."

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:24.050
Opposition leader Jeremy Corby was far from
convinced.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:28.140
The prime minister said nothing is agreed
until everything is agreed.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.030
It's clear from this document that nothing
is agreed.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:37.920
This is the blindfold Brexit we all feared,
a leap in the dark.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.530
German Chancellor Angela Merkel had threatened
to boycott Sunday's summit unless everything

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.740
was hammered out in advance.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.080
She now seems to be on board.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.490
I will do everything possible to get an exit
agreement.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:58.620
A disorderly Brexit is the worst possible
thing, not just for the economy but for our

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:04.200
future relationship.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:09.359
Also today, prime minister May had what she
said was a friendly chat with the Austrian

00:02:09.359 --> 00:02:12.420
Chancellor Sebastian Kurz.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:07:19.800
Getting the Brexit deal through her own parliament
will be an altogether more hostile process.

